      "text": "Huh. Interesting that he forsakes some very powerful and faithful men to David. More interesting is that he ignores Solomon specifically. What problem did he have with Solomon? Well, Nathan experienced quite a bit with Solomon and the whole issue with Bathsheba and David. He was there to give the bad news to David about her newborn baby. Solomon comes after the death of the firstborn to Bathsheba. He is a loved figure in the family of David. I don't think Solomon really thought of himself as the \"king\" type anyway, but it seems that Adonijah has a bit of a quarrel with Nathan and Solomon (and the mighty men of David) and so he refrains from inviting them. It is likely that he was planning to just take over the kingdom without requiring permission, especially when you find that he was purposely keeping specific key figures out of this dinner of acclamation.",
